ng equal would have been highly  inflationary and at the same time he  began adopting a practice that had been  talked about before and already adopted  by other central banks which is paying  interest on reserves which other things  being equal would have been deflationary  and so those two things have actually  offset each other why did he do that why  did he pay interest on reserves well  there are several reasons and some of  them are a little bit arcane but first  of all ever since deregulation and the  monetary measures began behaving more  erratically central banks have focused  on targeting interest rates and by  paying interest on reserves that  supposedly allows them to target  interest rates more closely then paying  interest on reserves is an idea that's  been around for a long time and even  Milton Friedman talked about it for  different reasons and it's a way of  getting banks to hold increasing  reserves and eliminating the tax that  reserve requirements are holding  reserves input so it was a way of  helping the banks and then it also is  has well I better not go into a bit the  clear the way the Fed runs the clearing  system they want banks holdem or more  reserved and especially in a crisis  where you're worried about them not  having enough money and all of that and  and and this this reflects an underlying  change in the nature of central banking  in the past central banking was about  just pumping money into the system  affecting the rate of inflation and  hopefully dampening the business cycle  which we know they didn't do but what  now what's now the the central bank has  become central banking has become the  new central planning central banks are  now interested in controlling interest  rates controlling the structure of  interest rates and controlling the  allocation of credit and if you  look at the asset side of the fed's  balance sheet all of the new assets that  they've acquired the fact that they're  engaging in this operation twist wh